Actually the reverse is true; "you" was more formal (well polite really) 
than "thee". From http://alt-usage-english.org/pronoun_paradigms.html:

        You may have been told that "thou" and "thee" were for familiar
        use, and "you" and "ye" were formal. This was not true
        originally, but it was true for about two centuries, roughly
        1450-1650, including Shakespeare's time. The previously plural
        "you" was used in the singular to signify politeness and
        respect, which left "thou" and "thee" for all the other 
        singular uses, ranging from endearing intimacy to bitter
        rudeness. Eventually, the politer "you" drove out nearly all
        uses of "thee" and "thou"; they survived mostly in poetry and
        religion. 

> Nobody talks that way anymore except for gods and
> such... It is like Thou is the "Siezen" form of You (the
> "duzen" form).

Well I'm no expert on German, but I would have said "thee" ought to be used 
more like German's "du" and "you" more like "sie". Of course, the fact it 
is only now used in poetic and particularly religious contexts[1] has 
obscured this.


Roger

[1] To confuse matters further, apparently the Authorised Version of the 
bible used (allegedly 100% faithfully) the already archaic strict plural-
singular distinction. As the most familiar example of this is the Lord's 
prayer, in which God the Father is addressed using "thy", some confusion 
has resulted. The AV itself does not restrict use of "thee" to God - Jesus 
addresses Peter as "thee" for example.

